course: dictionary
lesson: 13608
title: Incur: bringing trouble on yourself
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Incur is a formal word, the kind you find in letters, rules and money problems. It means something bad, such as a cost or a fine, comes to you because of what you did. «If you park on the yellow lines, you will incur a fine.» The word «fine» tells you that money must be paid, and the word «incur» tells you the fine comes from parking there.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «The flood incurred huge costs for the town.» Nobody wants a flood, and the town must now spend money it did not plan to spend. So incur means to have a cost or loss come to you, usually when you did not want it.
explain: Incur can also mean making someone angry. «Her rude joke incurred the teacher's anger.» The joke caused the anger, so incur means to bring something bad on yourself, not something good.
explain: Incur changes its form like other verbs. You can say «incurs», «incurred» or «incurring». Today we say «incurred» for something that already happened: «The company incurred a loss last year.»
fact: Incur means to have something bad happen to you because of what you did.
fact: Incur often goes with money words such as costs, fines, debts and losses.
fact: «Incur» is a formal word, so you will mostly see it in letters, rules and business reports.
fact: «Incurred» is the past form, as in «She incurred a penalty for being late.»
fact: Look for clues like «fine», «cost» or «anger» around the word to work out its meaning.
exercise: «If you park on the yellow lines, you will incur a fine.» What does incur mean here? | To wash the car before you leave | To find a free parking space | To lose the car keys | *To bring a fine upon yourself, because of what you did
exercise: «The flood incurred huge costs for the town.» What does incur suggest? | Nobody noticed anything at all | *Money had to be spent, and the town did not want to spend it | Money was found on the street | The town got a free gift
exercise: «Her rude joke incurred the teacher's anger.» Find and copy the word that means brought on or caused. | joke | teacher's | rude | *incurred
test: «Sam kept his library book too long, so he incurred a fee.» What does incurred mean here? | Read the book twice | Forgot his own name | *Had a fee come to him, because of what he did | Gave a gift to the library
test: Which word means the same as incur, when a bad thing comes to you because of what you did? | *Bring upon yourself | Celebrate | Forget | Avoid
exam: Which sentence uses incur correctly? | Mum incurred a lovely surprise party for us | We incurred a sunny day at the beach | *The hotel will incur extra charges if we check out late | I incur my dinner happily every night
